{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And they took… the son of Abram's brother…</b> The reason it was necessary to say ״the son of Abram's brother,״ and also the reason for its saying <b>and he was dwelling in Sodom</b> seemingly unnecessarily — for we already know that he had established his place there — is to convey that the kings knew that this Lot was the son of Abram's brother, and despite this they took him and led him away into captivity. And Scripture gives the reason why they did not fear Abraham's honor — for he was esteemed and exalted in the eyes of the whole world — namely that he was dwelling in Sodom. This is along the lines of what Amram, of blessed memory, said: ״Moab was purged through Sichon״ — end quote. That is, even though HaShem exempted Moab from Israel's hand (Deuteronomy 2:9), once they were found under Sichon's rule it became permitted to them. So too Lot: even though he was the brother's son of a man at peace with them, nevertheless, once he was found in Sodom it became permitted to them. And in this we find satisfaction for the question of Abraham's conduct: why he did not first call for peace with the four kings to get Lot back, and only if they refused would he strike them as he did — rather, the reason is that it was known to them that Lot was Abraham's nephew, and therefore Scripture hinted at this by saying <b>And he heard… that his brother was taken captive</b> — meaning, he was taken captive after it was known that he was Abraham's brother, and in this they revealed that they were enemies of Abraham, and therefore he pursued them and struck them." "<b>And if</b> you say: but the judgment is the same with the four kings who took him from within a city that was in rebellion against them. And it may be answered that what we say — ״they were purged through Sichon״ — applies to a nation with which we have neither peace nor war, but men of peace we do not judge as subject to captivity. And go forth and learn from what Israel did to the Kenite, concerning whom they were commanded (I Samuel 15:6): ״Depart, go down from among the Amalekite״ etc.; and likewise they should have commanded him to move out from among them if they were men of peace with Abraham."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:12.517639752Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}